  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "A roof in Utah typically lasts between 15 and 30 years for asphalt shingles, while metal or tile roofs can endure for 40 to 70 years or more.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "between 15 and 30 years for asphalt shingles, while metal or tile roofs can endure for 40 to 70 years or more"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Utah's intense UV rays, heavy snow loads, and rapid freeze-thaw cycles can cause local roofs to wear out faster than national averages."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Lifespan by Roofing Material"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "3-Tab Asphalt Shingles: Lasts 15 to 20 years. These budget-friendly options have a thinner build and struggle the most under extreme temperature swings ."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Architectural Shingles: Lasts 20 to 30 years. These thicker, multi-layered shingles handle Utah weather much better. Premium options with proper attic ventilation can push past 30 years."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Metal Roofing: Lasts 40 to 70 years. Standing seam and steel systems shed heavy snow easily and resist UV damage, making them top performers in mountain climates."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Concrete or Clay Tile: Lasts 50 years or more. Extremely durable in dry conditions, though freeze-thaw cycles require sound installation to prevent cracking."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Why Utah Climate Ages Roofs Faster"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "High UV Exposure: Intense sunlight at high elevations breaks down the asphalt and granules on standard shingles much faster."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Freeze-Thaw Cycles: Melting snow trickles into tiny crevices at night, refreezes, and expands, which lifts and cracks materials."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Heavy Snow & Ice Dams: Accumulated snow creates moisture traps along the eaves if attic ventilation is poor, leading to leaks and rotted decking."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you want to evaluate your specific situation, tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What material is your current roof made of?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "How old is the roof right now?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you look out for warning signs or decide if it's time for an inspection."
      }
    ],
